<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eshnunna</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eshnunna was an ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> in the Diyala valley, east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>, known for its law code. It was a rival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and was finally destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> of the Babylonian Empire around 1755 BCE. Its location near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> also tied it to the politics of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eshnunna.jpg" alt="Eshnunna" width="320" height="336" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marie-Lan Nguyen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Woman_head_Tell_Asmas_Louvre_AO12434.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Eshnunna was an ancient city and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, in the Diyala Valley north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a> proper but securely within the Sumerian cultural milieu. Its modern site is Tell Asmar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. The city's tutelary deity was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tishpak">Tishpak</a>, though Sin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Adad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inanna">Inanna</a> of Kiti were also worshipped there. Because it controlled trade routes it was a gateway between Mesopotamian and Elamite culture, with access to horses from the north, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> and other metals and precious stones. A pendant of copal from Zanzibar was found in a grave there, and a small number of seals and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley civilisation</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Inhabited since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jemdet-nasr-period">Jemdet Nasr period</a>, around 3000 BCE, Eshnunna was a major city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-dynastic-period">Early Dynastic period</a>. Its first known rulers were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> governors under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a>. Ituria, one of them, built a temple to the deified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shu-sin">Shu-Sin</a> in a new lower town. His son Šu-ilišu in 2026 BCE replaced the Ur III calendar with a local one and styled himself king instead of governor.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> fell, Eshnunna was for about fifteen years the independent state of Warum. Ishbi-Erra of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isin">Isin</a> defeated an invader from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subartu">Subartu</a> around 2010 BCE and installed Nur-ahum as ruler. Kirikiri then ousted Nur-ahum and founded a line with his son Bilalama, who gave his daughter Me-Kubi in marriage to Tan-Ruhuratir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> and tried to stay on good terms both with Elam and with Ilum-muttabbil of Der, though the two were at war. A fire later destroyed the palace, perhaps after a foreign invasion. After the city was captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> it was ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> governors under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. In the 12th century BCE the Elamite ruler Shutruk-Nakhunte conquered it and carried statues from the site to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The ruins form the mound of Tell Asmar, about 50 miles north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. A team of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oriental-institute">Oriental Institute</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-chicago">University of Chicago</a> led by Henri Frankfort excavated it in six seasons between 1930 and 1936. They concentrated on the palace and the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shu-sin">Shu-Sin</a> and on the Abu Temple, whose phases helped to define the three parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-dynastic-period">Early Dynastic period</a>. The Tell Asmar Hoard, twelve gypsum temple statues in a simplified geometric style with greatly enlarged inlaid eyes, was found in its Square Temple.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laws-of-eshnunna">Laws of Eshnunna</a>, found on two tablets at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tell-harmal">Shaduppum</a> (Tell Harmal) and a fragment at Tell Haddad, were written around the reign of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadusha">Dadusha</a>. They are similar to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi</a>, though the date when the laws were composed is unknown.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eshnunna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hammurabi destroyed the city of <b>Eshnunna</b> not by storming it but by damming and then releasing floodwater against it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Sukkalmah period, around 1765 BCE, the sukkalmah Siwe-Palar-Khuppak led a coalition that conquered <b>Eshnunna</b>, and rulers of the stature of Hammurabi of Babylon and Zimri-Lim of Mari addressed him as father, a term denoting superior rank.</blockquote><a href="/empire/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After Hammurabi had defeated Elam, <b>Eshnunna</b> and Larsa with the help of Mari’s troops, relations between the two kings broke down, perhaps over control of the Euphrates trade and the towns between their kingdoms.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mari">Mari · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Hammurabi waited until Rim-Sin grew old, conquered his territory in one swift campaign, and later betrayed allies in <b>Eshnunna</b>, Elam and Mari to gain their territories, securing Babylonian dominance over the Mesopotamian plain through military prowess, diplomacy and treachery.</blockquote><a href="/work/code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
